sec.p:
This occurred in a case in Buffalo, New York, that arose in the mid-1980s. A court-appointed expert testified as to his understanding of what constitutes child abuse:
\textsc{lawyer for mother:}
If a seven year old consented to sexual touching, then that would not be sexual abuse in your definition?
\textsc{psychologist:}
I would say thatâs inappropriate. I would not call that sexual abuse.

Louisiana attorney and national child abuse advocate Richard Ducote, in a memorandum on this case addressed to the Cobb County Bar Association and to the Metro Atlanta Board of Mental Health Professionals, alleged that the expert had been misled by the childâs law guardian, her “good friend,” who had “assured her that the sexual abuse allegations were false.” Once led to believe (through this unofficial persuasion) that the charges could be ignored, the evaluator found many of the criteria of Richard Gardnerâs Parental Alienation Syndrome present in the two girls. In her report claiming an “emergency,” she noted “the following features of parental alienation”:
a campaign of denigration against [the father];
weak, frivolous or absurd reasons were given for the deprecating feelings and statements;
a lack of normal ambivalence toward the parents (all good and all bad);
childâs opinions are independently initiated [in one child only];
automatic support for the favored parent;
almost complete lack of empathy for the feelings of the alienated parent; and
borrowed scenarios or “adult wording.”

Yet, despite this evidence, the case turned sharply against the mother once the court appointed a mental health expert to assess the validity of the sexual abuse claims. After conducting six interviews with the mother and her children, he wrote in his report: “I also find the mother utilizing numerous exclusionary maneuvers and she is presenting the father as inconsequential and unimportant [to the children].” His testimony, in which he invoked Gardnerâs PAS theory, was harsh and clear:
\textsc{mental health expert:}
My recommendation is that all the children should be placed immediately in the fatherâs custody, and that the mother should have absolutely no contact with these children for thirty days. fatherâs counsel: You are basing that on what concept that you are aware of?
\textsc{mental health expert:}
Well, this is part of this Parental Alienation Syndrome and my observations and my opinion is that these children do not hate their father and thatâs one of the prime symptoms of Parental Alienation Syndrome is that the children hate their father. Children are not born with the gene to hate their father. So I see that all this alienation thatâs been going on by the mother has not worked. The father has been persistent in trying to get contact with these children, when it hasnât worked, now we have the atom bomb. This is right out of textbooks.
\textsc{fatherâs counsel:}
So now you are saying since what other maneuvers mother might have gone through, the newest one is the allegation of sexual abuse [which] is just a new twist on the alienation syndrome?
\textsc{mental health expert:}
This is an example of what Dr Gardner calls escalating of more bizarre, more unusual allegations to try to get the father out of the childrenâs lives and when these donât work, they get stranger and more bizarre. Then they still donât work, then this is one sure way to get a father out of the childrenâs lives absolutely.
